  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Community Based HIV&amp;AIDS prevention, care and mitigation initiatives in South Sudan-Juba &amp; Terekeka</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This is an 11 month bridging gap project as CAFOD continues to finalise write up of its new country strategy paper. The project is a continuation of the support that CAFOD has been providing to the diocese to address the HIV and AIDS situation whilst also complimenting other HIV and AIDS initiatives through raising community awareness around HIV and AIDS. The project is developed building on key recommendations from the 2013 HIV&amp;AIDS final evaluation report and it is developed to target church structures in the Archdiocese of Juba with the main aim of strengthening its capacity in responding to the needs of PLWHAs while engaging in HIV&amp;AIDS awareness campaigns at parish level so as to increase risk perceptions in selected church primary and secondary schools in order to reduce and prevent infection and re-infection, most especially among the youth and teenagers. The project is going to provide support to religious leaders as well as diocesan staff, people living with HIV&amp;AIDS and other diocesan level actors in the response against HIV&amp;AIDS and sustainable livelihoods support through horticulture and income generating activities to PLWHAs to address issues related to income/asset building and nutrition. The project also aims to promote positive living and abstinence as well as educating the communities around issues to do with HIV and AIDS. Implementation of the project will therefore support training of 160 peer educators whom among others shall include teachers and to reach out to at least 3,500 at risk youth and teenager population in six primary schools and four secondary schools targeted in the parishes of Juba Archdiocese and finally to support livelihoods and income generation initiatives of 140 PLWHA through support in provision of agricultural inputs (vegetable seeds and tools) as well as IGA support through cash grants and business skills training to the targeted beneficiaries.</narrative>
  </description>
